<!-- 4. WHAT IS TRANSLATION? -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> What is “translation”?</h3>
    <div class="question-box">How does high-level source code become instructions a CPU can execute?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment">A CPU ultimately executes <span class="keyword" data-def="Binary instructions executed directly by a CPU (architecture-specific).">machine code</span>.</p>
        <p class="fragment">A translator converts <span class="keyword" data-def="Human-readable program written in a high-level programming language.">source code</span> into a form that can be executed: machine code, <span class="keyword" data-def="Intermediate code designed to be executed by a virtual machine (VM), not directly by a CPU.">bytecode</span>, or immediate execution.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="fragment box-blue">
        <p class="small-text"><strong>Connection to A1.1:</strong> those machine instructions are executed via the fetch–decode–execute cycle.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 5. INTERPRETERS -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Program that translates and executes source code line-by-line at runtime.">Interpreters</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">What changes when a program is translated “as it runs”?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> translate and execute <strong>line-by-line</strong> (or statement-by-statement).</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Strength:</strong> fast feedback during development.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Weakness:</strong> slower execution (translation overhead each run).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-bug"></i> Error detection</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Errors are found at <strong>runtime</strong> when the problematic line is reached.</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Some errors may hide until rarely executed code paths occur.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 6. COMPILERS -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Program that translates source code into machine code (or an intermediate form) before execution.">Compilers</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">What changes when the whole program is translated before it runs?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Mechanism:</strong> translate the entire program to <span class="keyword" data-def="Output of compilation, typically machine code that may need linking to form an executable.">object code</span> / <span class="keyword" data-def="Machine-code program file that can be run directly by the operating system/CPU.">executable</span>.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Strength:</strong> fast execution once compiled.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>Weakness:</strong> compile step slows iteration during development.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-shield"></i> Error detection</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Compilers catch syntax and some semantic errors <strong>before</strong> execution.</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Programs typically won’t run until errors are fixed.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 7. BYTECODE + VM -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Intermediate code designed to be executed by a virtual machine (VM), not directly by a CPU.">Bytecode</span> &amp; <span class="keyword" data-def="Software that runs bytecode by emulating/exposing a platform-independent runtime environment.">Virtual Machines</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">Can we get portability without giving up performance completely?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment">Some languages compile to <span class="keyword" data-def="Intermediate code designed to be executed by a virtual machine (VM), not directly by a CPU.">bytecode</span> (an intermediate representation).</p>
        <p class="fragment">A <span class="keyword" data-def="Software that runs bytecode by emulating/exposing a platform-independent runtime environment.">virtual machine (VM)</span> runs the bytecode on different platforms.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box fragment">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-globe"></i> Benefit</h4>
            <p class="small-text">“Write once, run anywhere” (with the correct VM installed).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue fragment">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-gauge-high"></i> Cost</h4>
            <p class="small-text">If bytecode is interpreted, execution can be slower than native machine code.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 8. JIT -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Runtime compilation that converts frequently executed bytecode (“hot spots”) into machine code for speed.">Just‑In‑Time (JIT) compilation</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">How can a system become faster while it runs?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment">Start by running <span class="keyword" data-def="Intermediate code designed to be executed by a virtual machine (VM), not directly by a CPU.">bytecode</span> on a VM.</p>
            <p class="fragment">Identify frequently executed “hot spots”.</p>
            <p class="fragment">Compile hot spots into <span class="keyword" data-def="Binary instructions executed directly by a CPU (architecture-specific).">machine code</span> and cache them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-fire"></i> Why it works</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Long-running programs benefit because hot paths repeat many times.</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Browsers use JIT to accelerate JavaScript execution.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 9. COMPARISON TABLE -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> Evaluating Translation Strategies</h3>
    <div class="question-box">Which approach is “best” depends on context — what trade-offs matter?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <table style="width:100%; font-size:0.85em;">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Interpreter</th><th>Compiler</th><th>Bytecode + VM (+ JIT)</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Startup</strong></td><td>Fast</td><td>Slower (compile first)</td><td>Medium</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Execution speed</strong></td><td>Often slower</td><td>Often fastest</td><td>Improves with JIT</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Portability</strong></td><td>High (needs interpreter)</td><td>Lower (per-architecture)</td><td>High (needs VM)</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Error detection</strong></td><td>Runtime</td><td>Before runtime</td><td>Mixed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Typical use</strong></td><td>Scripting, rapid iteration</td><td>Systems, performance-critical</td><td>Cross-platform apps</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 10. QUICK CHECKS -->
<section data-background="#fff3e0">
    <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
    <div class="question-box">A safety-critical embedded system needs predictable performance. Which approach is most suitable?</div>
    <div class="fragment box-blue">
        <p><strong>Compilation to native machine code</strong> — predictable execution without runtime translation overhead.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section data-background="#fff3e0">
    <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
    <div class="question-box">You want fast experimentation and immediate feedback while learning. Which approach is most suitable?</div>
    <div class="fragment box-blue">
        <p><strong>Interpretation</strong> — you can run code immediately and see errors as the program executes.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 11. MISCONCEPTION CHECK -->
<section>
    <section>
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-check-double"></i> Misconception Check</h2>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A.</strong> Compilers find errors at runtime when the bad line is executed.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B.</strong> Interpreted programs can start running without translating the whole source code first.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C.</strong> Bytecode + VM improves portability.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D.</strong> JIT compilation can improve performance by compiling hot spots to machine code.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-text" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.6em;">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-down blue"></i> <span class="blue">Press Down</span> for answers
        </p>
    </section>

    <section data-background="#f4f6f8">
        <h3>Answers</h3>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A: False.</strong> Compilers detect many errors before execution.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B: True.</strong> Interpretation allows immediate execution.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C: True.</strong> Bytecode runs wherever the VM exists.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D: True.</strong> JIT compiles frequently used code paths at runtime.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 12. SUMMARY -->
<section data-background="linear-gradient(135deg, #0E214B 0%, #1a3a7a 100%)">
